 | `WriteAgentInstructions` | `bool` | `true` | When `true`, writes byte-identical `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` files into the reports directory, telling an AI agent to read `Failures.md` and use [`kronikol query`](Querying-Reports) rather than open the report. Static text — no scenario names, no captured content — because an instruction file carrying attacker-influenceable text is a prompt-injection channel. Since 3.1.0. |
+| `WriteQueryScript` | `bool` | `true` | When `true`, writes `query.cs` into the reports directory beside a JSON data file (3.32.0): `kronikol query` for a machine where the tool is not installed and cannot be, such as an AI agent's session that may not install tools or reach the NuGet feed. From the reports directory, `dotnet run --file query.cs -- summary .` prints what `kronikol query summary .` prints, because it runs the same engine: the `Kronikol` that wrote the report, loaded from the test run's output or the NuGet cache. It restores and downloads nothing, and needs the .NET 10 SDK. Never written into a folder a C# project would compile (below one holding a `.csproj`, outside its `bin`, its `obj` and any folder whose name starts with a dot, such as `.logs/kronikol/`, unless the project leaves the folder out); the run records an `OptionNotApplied` diagnostic instead. A `query.cs` Kronikol did not write is left alone. See [Querying Reports](Querying-Reports#with-nothing-installed-and-no-network-querycs-3320) |
 | `WriteRunSummaryToConsole` | `bool` | `true` | When `true`, the run prints one closing block naming the reports directory, the size of the data file, the failing scenarios and the `kronikol query failures` command that explains them. Paths, counts, `stableId`s and scenario names only — never a message, a URI or a body, because CI logs are read far more widely than artifacts. On GitHub Actions a matching `::notice` annotation follows. The console is a best-effort channel rather than a guaranteed one: measured on .NET 10, every VSTest-hosted runner under `dotnet test` swallows what a library writes from a run-end hook unless verbosity is `detailed`, and TUnit's runner suppresses it at any verbosity — which is why `GenerateFailuresDigest` and `WriteAgentInstructions` write *files*. Since 3.1.0. |
 | `SetupHighlightColor` | `string` | `"#F6F6F6"` | Background colour for the setup partition when `SeparateSetup` and `HighlightSetup` are both `true`. |
 | `ExpectedTestCount` | `Func<int>?` | `null` | When set, report generation is skipped if the actual test count is less than the expected count. Guards against partial test runs overwriting complete specifications. |
